{"command": "wc -l /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md; head -60 /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "271 /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md\n# Feature Specification: Atomic Batch Transactions for TinyDB\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-transactions`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-17\n\n**Status**: Draft\n\n**Input**: User description: \"Build tinydb.journal.BatchWriter(db), wrapping a supplied TinyDB instance without changing its ordinary APIs. Use the default table only. apply(operations) accepts a list of operation dictionaries (insert/remove), returns inserted document IDs, and is atomic: on any error the complete pre-batch default-table data is restored. Ordinary TinyDB reads/writes and preexisting documents continue to work; inserted and returned nested data must not alias the caller's input.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Apply a batch of inserts and removes atomically (Priority: P1)\n\nA caller with a TinyDB instance supplies a batch of operations (insert a\ndocument, or remove a document by its ID) to `BatchWriter.apply` and\ngets back the list of IDs of the documents that were inserted, in\noperation order. If every operation is valid, the default table ends up\nwith exactly the expected documents.\n\n**Why this priority**: This is the core value of the feature; without it\nthere is nothing to use. It is independently deliverable: a caller can\nrun a fully valid batch and observe the resulting table.\n\n**Independent Test**: Start from a known table state, call `apply` with a\nmix of valid insert and remove operations, and verify (a) the returned\nID list matches the inserted documents in order, (b) the default table\ncontains exactly the expected documents afterwards, and (c) removes\ncontribute no entries to the returned list.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** an empty default table, **When** `apply` is called with\n   `[]`, **Then** it returns `[]` and the table remains empty.\n2. **Given** an empty default table, **When** `apply` is called with one\n   insert operation, **Then** it returns a single-element list whose\n   entry is the ID of the inserted document, and the table contains\n   that document.\n3. **Given** a table containing documents with known IDs, **When**\n   `apply` is called with a remove operation for an existing ID, **Then**\n   the document is removed and the returned list is `[]`.\n4. **Given** any valid batch mixing inserts and removes, **When**\n   `apply` succeeds, **Then** the returned list contains exactly the\n   inserted IDs, in operation order, and the final default-table state\n   equals the pre-batch state plus inserts minus removes.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Roll back the whole batch on any error (Priority: P1)\n\nA caller submits a batch in which some operation is invalid (unknown\noperation kind, malformed fields, or a remove targeting a missing ID).\nThe whole batch fails: the default table is restored to its complete\npre-batch state, including documents inserted earlier in the same batch\nand documents removed earlier in the same batch.\n\n**Why this priority**: Atomicity is the defining property of this\nfeature; a partial application would silently corrupt the caller's data\nand defeat the purpose of batching. It is independently testable with a\nbatch whose later operation is invalid.\n"}